TY  - JOUR
AU  - Kaur, M
TI  - Medical image captioning: A systematic review of methods, evaluation, trustworthiness, and clinical translation
T2  - COMPUTER SCIENCE REVIEW
AB  - Medical image captioning (MIC) aims to generate clinically meaningful reports from medical images by inte grating visual understanding with natural language generation. Despite rapid methodological progress from encoder-decoder and transformer-based models to knowledge-guided, vision-language pretrained, and recent large multimodal model (LMM)-driven approaches, improvements in benchmark performance have not trans lated into clinically reliable and trustworthy systems. This paper presents a PRISMA-guided systematic review of MIC research published from 2015 to 2025, with primary emphasis on advances reported since 2020. The liter ature is synthesized across six complementary dimensions: architectural design, learning paradigm, knowledge integration, modality and clinical domain, output structure, and evaluation and clinical translation. The review shows that modern models improve report fluency, multimodal alignment, and generative flexibility, but they remain limited by weak visual grounding, susceptibility to hallucination, heavy reliance on lexical evaluation metrics that poorly reflect diagnostic correctness, and strong concentration on chest radiography with limited generalization across modalities and institutions. Explainability, uncertainty estimation, and human-centered validation are increasingly recognized as essential requirements, yet they are still reported inconsistently across the literature. Furthermore, most published evidence remains retrospective and single-site, leaving a substantial gap between benchmark performance and deployment readiness. Future progress in MIC will depend on clinically grounded evaluation, explicit visual-textual evidence alignment, uncertainty-aware reasoning, broader cross-site validation, and human-centered deployment frameworks that support assisted rather than autonomous clinical reporting.
