AU  - Zhang, YK
AU  - Wang, GS
AU  - Xu, P
TI  - Sketch-to-Energy model automation with multimodal LLMs and retrieval-augmented generation
T2  - ENERGY AND BUILDINGS
AB  - Building Energy Modeling (BEM) is critical for improving building energy efficiency. However, modeling in the early architectural design stages is often hindered by challenges such as extracting geometric information from sketches and configuring complex parameters. To address these challenges and enable efficient energy analysis in the early design phases, we propose an automated BEM method. Users only need to provide preliminary architectural floor plan sketches and modeling requirements in natural language, after which our method can automatically generate complete simulation models. The proposed method features two major innovations. First, we leverage multimodal large language models (multimodal LLMs) and computer vision techniques to generate structured 3D building geometry from hand-drawn sketches. The multimodal LLM denoises errors in sketches and extracts room functions and dimensions, while computer vision methods generate wall and window coordinates. Second, we employ a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) technique to retrieve similar cases within the same ASHRAE climate zone from an EnergyPlus model database. The BEM parameters of these similar cases are used as prompts to guide the LLM in generating thermal parameters and operational schedules that align with real-world scenarios. The effectiveness of the method was validated through four test cases with diverse morphologies across three ASHRAE climate zones. Quantitative results demonstrate robust geometric reconstruction capabilities, achieving a mean wall length error of 0.05 m and a room function classification accuracy of 91.2%. The thermal and operational parameters generated are empirically grounded, resulting in simulation models that exhibit high consistency with expert-built benchmarks (average R-2 > 0.995 for annual hourly loads). The average total modeling time was 8 min. Overall, the method provides an automated bridge between early architectural design and energy performance simulation in the demonstrated cases, enabling rapid conversion of conceptual sketches into executable models while supporting data-driven design optimization through customizable parameter retrieval weights.
